WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.050
This video is sponsored by LegalZoom.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:04.820
More about LegalZoom at the end of the video.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.640
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.530
For 20 Years, the kids of South Park have
been skewering every sacred cow they can find.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.699
And at Wisecrack, that's just the kind of
thing that gets us hot.

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:24.660
In its thirteenth season, South Park masterfully
merged two of its favorite targets for ridicule:

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.349
religion and consumerism.

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:30.740
And you all know what happens when we marry
God and money.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.900
OK, sure.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:40.860
But also a multilayered argument about personal
agency and the opaque nature of the economy.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.130
Break open a bag of Cheesy Poofs for this
Wisecrack Edition of the Philosophy of South

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:46.910
Park's "Margaritaville."

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:50.830
Religion has long been a handy tool for explaining
the unexplainable.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:54.780
It's through clever religious allegory, then,
that "Margaritaville" manages to tackle the

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:01.370
economic crash of 2008 and a mysterious system
that for many remains the unexplainable - the

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:02.370
economy.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:11.790
When the all-powerful "Economy" leaves South
Park in tatters, everyone is quick to direct

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:18.180
blame somewhere -- including Wall Street and
-- But it's Randy's characteristically bizarre

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:26.960
perspective that builds a large following
across South Park.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.850
Taking his cues from old-school Protestant
ethics.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:43.320
Randy preaches the economy is a petty vengeful
diety that must be placated by worship.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.360
Randy's position draws from the fearful punishment-oriented philosophies of the Old Testament.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.160
And South Park steers straight into those
biblical parallels, right down to the flinging

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.980
of stones -- I mean squirrels -- by those
who pass judgement on the fallen.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.780
In fact, Randy's persecution of Kyle mimics
the biblical role of the Pharisees.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:17.099
The Pharisses were an influential group of
people who, in the New Testament, greeted

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:24.019
Jesus's message with skepticism, paranoia,
and ultimately, incitement of violence.

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:26.909
Kyle's third-act self-sacrifice in which he,
a Christ-like figure, has dinner with his

00:02:26.909 --> 00:02:31.370
enemy, takes on all of South Park's debt,
and sacrafices himself for the community furthers

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:38.890
the biblical parallels that dominate the episode.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.849
Kyle's sacrafice parallels the crucifixion
of Jesus, but where Jesus took on the sins

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:45.790
of the world.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:50.489
Kyle shoulders the debt of all of South Park.

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:55.579
Just as Jesus's death was mourned by a weeping
Mary as he was taken from the Cross, Kyle's

00:02:55.579 --> 00:02:59.450
figurative "death" is mourned here by his
own weeping mother.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:00.530
Kyle has died.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:01.840
Kyle is risen.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.279
Kyle will come again.

00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:07.390
Not unlike the hero of the New Testament,
Kyle sets forth a philosphy which challenges

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.459
and chastises the accepted norms of the community.

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:18.400
But whereas Jesus set forth a religious code
of conduct, Kyle sets forth a financial one.

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:34.680
His philosphy, known in the real world as
faith-based economics proposes "the economy"

00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:38.040
is us -- and should be trusted, not feared.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.829
Since people drive the economy, Kyle argues,
we are at one with economy.

00:03:42.829 --> 00:03:44.209
Forever and ever.

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:45.209
Amen.

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:49.150
Margaritaville effectively positions the economy
as a modern religion unto itself.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:54.129
Throughout the episode, Randy and Kyle's conflicting
arguments don't merely demonstrate religious

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:58.450
parallels, They spring from some interesting
econmonic and social theory.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:03.569
For example, Randy's unforgiving message of
self-discpline and self-denial maps well with

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:08.419
the ethics described by German sociologist
Max Weber in "The Protestant Ethic and The

00:04:08.419 --> 00:04:09.889
Spirit of Capitalism".

00:04:09.889 --> 00:04:14.769
For Weber, the Protestant values of austerity
and frugality were crucial components in the

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:16.320
development of modern capitalism.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.720
In other words: work until you die, and don't
make any time for any fun.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.419
While Randy preaches economic restraint, Kyle
offers a very different take one reflected

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:30.270
in the work of British economist John Maynard
Keynes who proposed consumer spending is what

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.900
truly keeps the economy humming.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.990
If you want economic growth, Keynesian economics
argues, you've just gotta keep moving that

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:35.990
paper.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.530
But, hey, if you didn't know much about these
two economic perspectives, South Park is not

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:41.530
surprised.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.300
Because one of the big ideas tackled in this
episode is that economics are deliberately

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.669
made too difficult for most people to understand.

00:04:48.669 --> 00:04:53.440
This strategy of linguistically keeping information
at arm's length from the everyday citizen

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:59.440
is known as technostrategic discourse and
it's so powerful and so dangerous, "Margaritaville"

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.280
dedicates an entire "B" storyline to demonstrating
it in action.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:15.409
Take, for example, when Stan visits his bank
and is treated to a string of jargon before

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:19.080
his money magically disappears without explanation.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.639
Of course, we all know economics isn't magic,
but most of us don't quite know the intricacies

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:29.889
of what economics are.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:35.389
And, as the subprime mortgage crisis of the
late 2000s demonstrated, those who are in

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:39.370
the know are counting on, and even profiting
from, our ignorance.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:45.610
Even if they themselves might be ignorant,
too.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.919
French Philosopher and turtleneck enthusiast
Michel Foucault argued that, in our modern

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:56.219
era power was no longer concentrated but dispersed
through complex systems: prisions, religious

00:05:56.219 --> 00:05:59.659
authorities, and as South Park highlights,
finance.

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:04.319
Unlike traditional conceptions of power, which
might imagine a bunch of bankers in smoke-filled

00:06:04.319 --> 00:06:07.159
rooms conspiring to screw everyone over.

00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:12.550
Foucault explored how individuals each played
their own tiny role in this system of power,

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.259
often ignorant to complete workings of the
system.

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:19.770
Power, dispersed in this way, shows up in
South Park as Stan climbs up the ladder of

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.879
helpless bureaucrats: each of them powerless
to the rules, or magic, of the system they

00:06:24.879 --> 00:06:26.150
participate in.

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:31.830
In scene after scene, so-called experts sustain
and amplify power through the exploitation

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.640
of confusion.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.971
Stan goes to return his Margaritaville at
Sur le Table, only for Stan to be shooed off

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:50.620
to the finance company.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.790
The finance company points Stan to Wall St,
the people really running the show, only for

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:58.580
Stan to learn from Wall Street that it is
ultimately the treasury department who is

00:06:58.580 --> 00:06:59.830
in charge.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:05.259
The interesting piece here is that, Stan,
as he journeys into the world of finance to

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:14.229
find who is in charge finds out that no one
is really in charge.

00:07:14.229 --> 00:07:19.349
As the very nature of money is an amorphous
abstraction, its source -- and, with it, a

00:07:19.349 --> 00:07:22.210
source of power -- never seems to be anywhere.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:27.030
Anyone who's turned to a lawyer, to a doctor,
or to a computer technician for support probably

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.030
knows the drill.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:33.720
No matter how dense the language or how straightforward
the problem, you're simply expected to trust

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:34.949
in the experts.

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:37.780
But what's the result of this technocratic
babble?

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:42.840
According to South Park, the consumer is essentially
exhausted into submission, either willfully

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:48.120
throwing himself at the mercy of technocrats...or
uh, smashing dad's blender on the floor.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.789
In matters both relgious and economic, "Margaritaville"
challenges the seemingly distant and unknowable

00:07:53.789 --> 00:07:59.539
forces that govern our lives and fates.

00:07:59.539 --> 00:08:04.580
If money isn't real, if the powerful deliberately
complicate things in order to retain their

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:10.169
power, and if the answers we seek don't necessarily
lie with the experts or with the divine...

00:08:10.169 --> 00:08:15.740
whom does South Park suggest we blame in the
face of economic or spiritual misfortune?

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:21.389
Maybe the answer is found in the Jimmy Buffet
song "Margaritaville," in which a drunk man

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:24.479
sits in a bar and laments his failed relationship.

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:28.620
By the end of the tune, the man gradually
realizes his own drinking problem may have

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:34.230
contributed to his failure in love.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.520
Personal responsibility.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.280
It's a hell of a drug.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:46.400
Bottoms up, Wisecrack.

